.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt VFORK 2
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm vfork
.Nd create a new process without copying the address space
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft pid_t
.Fn vfork void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn vfork
creates a new process, as
.Xr fork 2
does, except that the child does not get a copy of the caller's address
space: it runs in the caller's own memory, on the caller's stack, until it
calls
.Xr execve 2
or
.Xr _exit 2 .
Until then the calling thread is suspended.
The child is otherwise a process of its own: it has its own copy of the
file descriptor table, its own credentials and signal dispositions, and
other threads of the parent keep running.
.Pp
Every change the child makes to memory is the parent's: its stores, the
program break it moves with
.Xr malloc 3 ,
the mappings and System V shared memory it attaches.
The child must not return from the function that called
.Fn vfork ,
since the parent's stack frame is still in use, and should call
.Xr _exit 2
rather than
.Xr exit 3
if its
.Xr execve 2
fails, so as not to flush the parent's
.Xr stdio 3
buffers.
.Pp
A caught signal sent to the suspended parent stays pending until the
child has exec'd or exited; a signal that terminates the parent, such as
.Dv SIGKILL ,
does so at once, and the child keeps the memory.
While it waits, the parent's state is
.Ql D
with the wait channel
.Ql ppwait ,
and the child's
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /status
shows
.Ql PPWait: 1 .
If the system runs out of memory, the parent and the child are one
address space to the OOM killer, and are killed together.
.Pp
.Xr posix_spawn 3
creates its child the same way, through
.Fn clone
with
.Dv CLONE_VM | CLONE_VFORK .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
In the child,
.Fn vfork
returns 0; in the parent, once the child has exec'd or exited, the child's
process ID.
On failure it returns \-1 in the parent, creates no child, and sets
.Va errno .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The system's process limit,
.Va kern.maxproc ,
has been reached.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
There was no memory for the new process's kernel stack.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr _exit 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr wait4 2 ,
.Xr posix_spawn 3
.Sh STANDARDS
.Fn vfork
was in
.St -p1003.1-2001
and was removed from later editions.
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn vfork
first appeared in
.Bx 3.0 .
In OxideBSD it was the same as
.Xr fork 2
until 0.3.0, which gave it its own system call and the shared address
space.
